I think this really will start to move off-topic quickly.  Remember,
your posts should be relavent to SELinux.  A general discussion of the
TCSEC, CC, assurance, or evaluation criteria is NOT on-topic.  I don't
have a good suggestion for where to take the discussion off the top of
my head.  So, I will suggest that people who have suggestions about
forums where the general evalution topics are appropriate let Jakob (not
the list) know.  As far as I'm concerned a message to the list from
Jakob with a considered summary about where those discussions should
take place (since those discussions could eventually be relevent to
SELinux) would still be on-topic.

On Wed, 2003-05-28 at 18:03, Jakob Oestergaard wrote:
> On Thu, May 15, 2003 at 04:06:32PM -0400, Howard Holm wrote:
> ...
> > Various
> > Protection Profiles including current C2 and B1 equivalents for
> > operating systems can be found at
> > http://www.nsff.org/protection_profiles/profiles.cfm
> 
> Hi Howard,
> 
> That was really helpful!
> 
> My apologies if this is moving off topic for the SELinux list - I do
> hope that this has some interest to SELinux people though...
> 
> I have been reading the TCSEC and CC documents (available from the CC
> web site), and I've been wondering to say the least...
> 
> It was my impression that CC was more or less a useless "commercialized"
> certification which really didn't say much about security - eg. Solaris
> 8 and Trusted Solaris 8 both have EAL 4, along with some version of
> windows by the way...
> 
> The site with profiles you linked to is useful (downloading documents
> now...) - but it only lists C2 and B1 -like profiles.  Is this the state
> of the art in today's world of computing, or are there more ambitious
> profiles out there somewhere?  If so, where?
> 
> When reading TCSEC, I get the feeling that someone was ambitious and
> forward thinking.  Not unreasonable at all.  I honestly like the idea
> (ok, dream) of an A1 certified system.
> 
> When seeing CC (and barely-C2 systems certified with the highest
> commonly recognized assurance level EAL4), I get the feeling that the
> world of computer security is going to the dogs...
> 
> In my eyes, what happened the past 18 years was that the idea of
> multilevel security and other useful (essential?) features were
> dropped... Now that it's needed more than ever...
> 
> Is my pessimistic view of the world close to the truth, or are there
> really B2/B3/A1 like profiles for CC available somewhere out there?
> 
> 
> Cheers,
